# Copy this file to `.env` and point it at your OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
#
# Run:  cp .env.example .env      (PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env)
#
# The app talks to exactly one endpoint. Both values below are read server-side
# only — see the note at the bottom.

# Base URL of your endpoint, WITHOUT the trailing /v1.
# The proxies append `/v1/models` and `/v1/chat/completions` to this, so with
# API_BASE=https://your-endpoint.example the app calls
# https://your-endpoint.example/v1/chat/completions
#
# Required. The dev server refuses to start without it, and the Vercel functions
# return a 500 naming this variable rather than guessing an upstream.
API_BASE=

# Sent by the proxy as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
# Leave empty only if your endpoint requires no authentication.
API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These names deliberately carry no VITE_ prefix, so Vite never inlines them
# into the browser bundle. They are read in exactly two places:
#   - vite.config.ts   (dev  — the /v1 dev-server proxy)
#   - api/v1/*.ts      (prod — the Vercel serverless proxy)
#
# Renaming either to VITE_API_BASE / VITE_API_KEY would ship your key to every
# visitor. Do not.
#
# `.env` is gitignored and is NOT deployed. For production, set both variables
# in the Vercel project: Settings -> Environment Variables.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
